  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(1,-4,2)$ and is perpendicular to the line that goes through the points \n$A(2,1,-3)$ and $B(1,6,-4).$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=\u27e81,-4,2\u27e9.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(2,1,-3)$ and $B(1,6,-4)$ we can find a vector parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\u27e81,6,-4\u27e9-\u27e82,1,-3\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-1,5,-1\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=\u27e8-1,5,-1\u27e9.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot \u27e8-1,5,-1\u27e9 & =\u27e81,-4,2\u27e9\\cdot \u27e8-1,5,-1\u27e9 \\\\ r\\cdot \u27e8-1,5,-1\u27e9 & =-23.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
